            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Setting Up Your Development Environment</h2>
                    <p>Before you can start building .NET applications, you need to install the .NET SDK (Software Development Kit). The SDK includes everything you need: the runtime, libraries, and command-line tools.</p>
                    
                    <p>In this lesson, we'll walk through installing .NET on Windows, macOS, and Linux, then verify the installation is working correctly.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>SDK vs Runtime</h2>
                    <p>It's important to understand the difference between the SDK and Runtime:</p>

                    <div class="comparison-grid">
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>.NET SDK</h4>
                            <p>Everything you need to <strong>develop</strong> applications. Includes the runtime, compilers, CLI tools, and libraries.</p>
                            <span class="tag recommended">Install This</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>.NET Runtime</h4>
                            <p>Only what's needed to <strong>run</strong> applications. Used on production servers where you don't need development tools.</p>
                            <span class="tag">Production Only</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Note</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>The SDK includes the runtime, so you only need to install the SDK for development. The runtime is installed automatically.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Installing on Windows</h2>
                    <p>There are several ways to install .NET on Windows:</p>

                    <h3>Option 1: Using the Installer (Recommended)</h3>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Visit <a href="https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download" target="_blank">dotnet.microsoft.com/download</a></li>
                        <li>Click "Download .NET SDK" for the latest version</li>
                        <li>Run the downloaded installer</li>
                        <li>Follow the installation wizard</li>
                    </ol>

                    <h3>Option 2: Using Windows Package Manager (winget)</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Install the latest .NET SDK
winget install Microsoft.DotNet.SDK.8

# Or install a specific version
winget install Microsoft.DotNet.SDK.9</code></pre>

                    <h3>Option 3: Using Chocolatey</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Install using Chocolatey
choco install dotnet-sdk</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Installing on macOS</h2>
                    <p>macOS users have these installation options:</p>

                    <h3>Option 1: Using the Installer</h3>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Visit <a href="https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download" target="_blank">dotnet.microsoft.com/download</a></li>
                        <li>Download the macOS installer (.pkg file)</li>
                        <li>Double-click to run and follow the prompts</li>
                    </ol>

                    <h3>Option 2: Using Homebrew (Recommended)</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Install the latest .NET SDK
brew install --cask dotnet-sdk

# Verify installation
dotnet --version</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Apple Silicon (M1/M2/M3)</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>.NET has native support for Apple Silicon. The installer will automatically detect your architecture and install the correct version.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Installing on Linux</h2>
                    <p>Linux installation varies by distribution:</p>

                    <h3>Ubuntu / Debian</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Add Microsoft package repository
wget https://packages.microsoft.com/config/ubuntu/22.04/packages-microsoft-prod.deb -O packages-microsoft-prod.deb
sudo dpkg -i packages-microsoft-prod.deb
rm packages-microsoft-prod.deb

# Install the SDK
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y dotnet-sdk-8.0</code></pre>

                    <h3>Fedora</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Install using dnf
sudo dnf install dotnet-sdk-8.0</code></pre>

                    <h3>Using the Install Script (Any Distribution)</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Download and run the install script
wget https://dot.net/v1/dotnet-install.sh -O dotnet-install.sh
chmod +x dotnet-install.sh
./dotnet-install.sh --channel 8.0

# Add to PATH (add to ~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc)
export DOTNET_ROOT=$HOME/.dotnet
export PATH=$PATH:$DOTNET_ROOT:$DOTNET_ROOT/tools</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Verifying Your Installation</h2>
                    <p>After installation, open a new terminal window and run these commands to verify everything is working:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Check the installed SDK version
dotnet --version

# See all installed SDKs and runtimes
dotnet --info

# List available SDKs
dotnet --list-sdks

# List available runtimes
dotnet --list-runtimes</code></pre>

                    <p>You should see output similar to:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">$ dotnet --version
8.0.100

$ dotnet --list-sdks
8.0.100 [/usr/share/dotnet/sdk]

$ dotnet --list-runtimes
Microsoft.AspNetCore.App 8.0.0 [/usr/share/dotnet/shared/Microsoft.AspNetCore.App]
Microsoft.NETCore.App 8.0.0 [/usr/share/dotnet/shared/Microsoft.NETCore.App]</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Installing an IDE</h2>
                    <p>While you can write .NET code in any text editor, a good IDE makes development much more productive:</p>

                    <div class="ide-options">
                        <div class="ide-card">
                            <h4>Visual Studio (Windows/Mac)</h4>
                            <p>The full-featured IDE from Microsoft. The Community edition is free for individuals and small teams.</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Best debugging experience</li>
                                <li>Built-in designers for UI</li>
                                <li>Comprehensive .NET support</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ide-card">
                            <h4>Visual Studio Code</h4>
                            <p>Lightweight, cross-platform editor. Free and highly extensible.</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Install the C# Dev Kit extension</li>
                                <li>Great for any platform</li>
                                <li>Fast and lightweight</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ide-card">
                            <h4>JetBrains Rider</h4>
                            <p>Cross-platform .NET IDE. Paid, but excellent for professional development.</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Powerful refactoring tools</li>
                                <li>Cross-platform</li>
                                <li>Great performance</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>VS Code Setup for .NET</h2>
                    <p>If you're using VS Code, follow these steps:</p>

                    <ol>
                        <li>Install VS Code from <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com" target="_blank">code.visualstudio.com</a></li>
                        <li>Open VS Code and go to Extensions (Ctrl+Shift+X)</li>
                        <li>Search for and install "C# Dev Kit"</li>
                        <li>The extension will automatically install required dependencies</li>
                    </ol>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Recommended Extensions</span>
                        </div>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>C# Dev Kit</strong> - Essential for .NET development</li>
                            <li><strong>NuGet Gallery</strong> - Browse and install packages</li>
                            <li><strong>.NET Core Test Explorer</strong> - Run tests from VS Code</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Troubleshooting Common Issues</h2>

                    <h3>'dotnet' is not recognized</h3>
                    <p>If you see this error, the .NET SDK isn't in your PATH:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Windows:</strong> Restart your terminal or computer</li>
                        <li><strong>macOS/Linux:</strong> Add the dotnet path to your shell profile</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3>Multiple SDK Versions</h3>
                    <p>You can have multiple .NET versions installed side by side. Use a <code>global.json</code> file to pin a specific version for a project:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "sdk": {
    "version": "8.0.100",
    "rollForward": "latestFeature"
  }
}</code></pre>

                    <h3>Permission Issues on Linux/Mac</h3>
                    <p>If you get permission errors, don't use <code>sudo</code> with dotnet commands. Instead, ensure your user owns the .dotnet directory:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">sudo chown -R $(whoami) ~/.dotnet</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Install the .NET SDK (not just the runtime) for development.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use <code>dotnet --info</code> to verify your installation and see all installed versions.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Multiple SDK versions can coexist—use global.json to pin versions per project.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>VS Code with C# Dev Kit is a great free option for .NET development.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
